#!/opt/bin/perl

=head1 NAME

mcp-vim - MCP server for live Vim editors

=head1 SYNOPSIS

   mcp-vim [--name NAME] [--bind ADDRESS[:PORT]] [--stdio] [--print-url]

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<mcp-vim> lets MCP clients read and edit buffers in running Vim editors,
including changes that have not been saved to disk. An assistant can work
with the same text you are editing, and its changes appear in Vim for you
to review and save.

It runs as a broker daemon. Each Vim editor needs the supplied Vim plugin,
which connects the editor to the broker. Your MCP client connects to the
same broker, which forwards requests to the editor and returns its replies.
The client can use MCP over HTTP or stdin/stdout; Vim always connects over
TCP using the plugin's private protocol.

One broker can serve several Vim editors. Each editor connects with a
target name, allowing the client to list the connected editors and choose
which one to work with. Buffer IDs belong to the selected editor.

See L</VIM PLUGIN INSTALLATION> for installing and connecting the Vim
plugin, and L<AnyEvent::MCP::Intro> for configuring an MCP client.

=head1 FIRST USE

Install the companion Vim plugin described below. Configure the MCP
client to launch:

   mcp-vim --stdio --name editor

In another terminal, under the same user and configuration directory,
retrieve the listener's credential-bearing URL:

   mcp-vim --name editor --print-url

Append a target name and enter the resulting command in Vim:

   :Mcp http://HOST:PORT/TOKEN/my-editor

Then ask the assistant to call C<targets>, C<state>, and C<read-lines>
for a buffer in C<my-editor>. C<targets> shows whether Vim connected;
C<state> supplies buffer IDs and changedticks. Reads include unsaved text.

Through a multibroker, tools have a prefix such as C<vim.targets>.
Start its mcp-vim child with C<--eager> so the listener exists before
the first tool call. Starting the broker alone does not connect Vim:
each editor must run C<:Mcp>. If connecting fails, check the broker's
C<--name>, bind address, token and configuration environment.
Edits require a current C<expected_changedtick>; they do not save files
until the separate C<save> tool or a writing Ex command is used.

=head1 TOOLS

C<targets> lists connected Vim sessions, and C<state> reports their buffers
and current cursor. C<read-lines> and C<grep> operate on live Vim buffers,
B<not> saved disk files. C<apply-diff> and C<substitute> edit buffers;
C<set-cursor> deliberately changes the visible editor position.
Use C<targets> first, then C<state> to obtain buffer IDs and changedticks.
A target identifies one connected editor; a buffer ID is local to that editor.

Editing operations require the buffer's C<expected_changedtick> from a
recent state or read. A changed tick rejects stale edits instead of silently
applying them to a buffer that has moved on. Diff context must also match.
Explicitly addressed unloaded buffers can be loaded on demand, so reads
and edits are not necessarily free of editor side effects. A buffer edit
does not save the file to disk; the user remains in control of that step.

=head2 Patch text and buffer lines

C<apply-diff> accepts Unicode text. The private JSON protocol decodes it
into a Vim String; the plugin writes buffer lines in Vim's internal
C<&encoding> and sends the patch String to GNU patch. C<&fileencoding>
and C<&fileformat> govern saving the buffer, not these temporary files.
The UTF-8 path has been checked with non-ASCII text.

Patching operates on complete logical lines, with a final newline,
independently of C<&endofline>. Nonempty output without that final newline
is rejected. Vim represents an embedded NUL in a buffer line as NL inside
the corresponding String; C<writefile>/C<readfile> preserve such bytes in
untouched lines.

NUL-containing diffs are rejected by the broker before permission prompting
or forwarding: Vim's C<json_decode> would drop C<\u0000> from Strings.
There is no raw-byte diff transport for Vim yet. This differs from the
byte-oriented shared helper and the filesystem tool's raw-byte base64 form.

=head2 Undo and redo

C<undo> and C<redo> each move one step through the selected buffer's normal
Vim undo tree. Both require C<target>, C<buffer> and C<expected_changedtick>,
and use the C<write> permission. The tick is checked after loading and
entering that buffer. A stale tick or an unmodifiable buffer rejects the
operation. Neither tool saves the buffer.

The result includes C<buffer>, the new C<changedtick>, C<changed>,
C<seq_cur> (the reached undo sequence), C<seq_last> (the latest allocated
sequence) and C<modified>. At the oldest/newest reachable state, the operation
succeeds with C<changed> false. Redo follows Vim's selected branch; it does
not switch to another branch or select an arbitrary historical sequence.

When another buffer is selected, the caller's original buffer and view are
restored. In the current buffer, Vim's normal undo/redo cursor adjustment
is retained. Undo acts on ordinary buffer history, including human edits;
it does not identify or group changes made by an assistant.

=head2 Open a file

C<open> accepts C<target> and a nonempty C<path> without NUL. It adds the
pathname to Vim's buffer list and loads it without selecting it. Relative
paths use Vim's current effective directory at execution time. The pathname
is passed to C<bufadd>, not an Ex command or shell; wildcard and command
characters are literal. A missing file becomes an empty named buffer.

An existing buffer is reused. Loaded contents, including unsaved edits,
are not reread from disk. The result reports C<buffer>, C<filename>,
C<changedtick>, C<line_count> and C<modified>, ready for subsequent tools.
No file is saved. Use C<set-cursor> separately to display the buffer.

This requires the separate C<open> action. Loading can run normal Vim
autocommands and modelines, which can change editor state or fail after
side effects. An error does not undo buffer creation or those effects.
Permission scopes still cover an editor, not an individual pathname.

=head2 Save modified buffers

C<save> accepts C<target>, C<buffer> and C<expected_changedtick>. It runs
Vim's C<:update> in that buffer: an unmodified buffer needs no write; a
modified buffer is saved to its current filename. There is no filename
argument, save-as or force option. A stale tick rejects the operation.

Saving requires the separate C<save> permission. A C<write> grant for
editing does not authorize saving. The result reports C<buffer>,
C<changedtick>, C<modified> and C<filename> after the command. Inspect
C<modified>: write autocommands can leave or make the buffer modified.

Normal Vim write checks and autocommands apply. They can change text,
rename or remove buffers, write other files, or fail after side effects.
The tick guard checks the buffer before the command; it does not constrain
autocommands or detect external changes to the disk file. A removed or
unloaded target after the command produces an error even if a write occurred.
The caller's original buffer and view are restored when possible.

=head2 Unrestricted Ex commands

C<ex> accepts C<target> and C<command>. Without C<buffer>, it executes the
command text in that editor's current buffer, window and directory.
An optional C<buffer> makes that buffer current first and leaves it current
unless the command changes it. C<expected_changedtick> requires C<buffer>
and is checked in Vim immediately before executing the command, after
entering the buffer. A stale tick prevents execution. Buffer loading and
entering can still have their normal Vim side effects.

For example, C<command =E<gt> "update"> with a buffer id and its latest
changedtick saves that buffer only if its contents have not changed since
you inspected it. The tick does not describe the disk file or prevent
autocommands from running.

The command can change the buffer, window and directory,
access files, invoke shell commands or configured functions, and exit Vim.
It is not restricted to the current buffer. Prefer the dedicated tools
when they express the requested operation.

The permission prompt includes the command text. Persistent choices cover
this exact command in this editor, any Ex command in this editor, or any
Ex command in all current and future editors. Exact means the complete
command string, including whitespace and newlines. Such a rule applies to
all buffers in that editor: approving C<update> is not approval for only
the buffer used in the original request. Commands can invoke mappings,
functions and autocommands whose behaviour changes independently of the
command text.

The result is C<{output: STRING}>, containing captured Vim messages.
An error is returned as a tool error; effects before that error remain.
Use noninteractive commands. Message capture suppresses ordinary message
display, but does not prevent commands from prompting or blocking Vim.
Request cancellation stops waiting for the reply, not a command already
running in Vim. If a command exits Vim, the connection can close before
a reply is delivered.

=head1 VIM PLUGIN INSTALLATION

The Vim plugin is included in the AnyEvent::MCP source distribution as
C<vim/plugin/mcp-vim.vim>. Installing the Perl distribution does not install
the Vim plugin. From the source distribution, install it yourself:

   mkdir -p ~/.vim/plugin
   cp vim/plugin/mcp-vim.vim ~/.vim/plugin/mcp-vim.vim

Alternatively, download the published plugin:

   mkdir -p ~/.vim/plugin
   wget -O ~/.vim/plugin/mcp-vim.vim \
      https://dist.schmorp.de/mcp-vim/1/mcp-vim.vim

The C</1/> is the major version of the private Vim/broker protocol, not
the MCP protocol or the Perl distribution. Restart Vim after installing
or updating the plugin.

Connect a Vim instance with C<:Mcp URL>, appending the desired target name
to the broker URL (which C<mcp-vim --print-url> can show):

   :Mcp http://HOST:PORT/TOKEN/TARGET

To avoid typing the URL prefix repeatedly, configure
C<g:mcp_broker_url> in your vimrc:

   let g:mcp_broker_url = 'http://HOST:PORT/TOKEN/'

Then use C<:Mcp TARGET>, with the target name alone.

Distinct Vim instances requesting the same target name receive suffixes
such as C< #2>. Reconnecting the same instance replaces its connection and
keeps its assigned name. Use C<targets> to obtain the actual names.

The Vim plugin uses a private protocol, not MCP. Its initial raw JSON line is
C<["mcp-vim", cookie, major, minor, target, instance_id]>; subsequent messages
use Vim's ordinary JSON channel envelope. The broker accepts clients with the
same major version and a minor version no newer than its own. Keep the broker
and plugin versions compatible when updating them.

The broker itself can connect to a multibroker using
C<--connect http://host:port/token/alias>. Its own TCP listener remains active
for Vim and browser connections. The multibroker URL and the Vim broker's own
listener URL have independent credentials.

=head1 CONNECTIONS AND AUTHENTICATION

By default, the broker derives a stable port in the 16384..32767 range
from its service-instance name, defaulting to C<default>. By default
its token is derived from the private global AnyEvent::MCP master
secret. Configured C<tokens> or repeated C<--token> options replace that
default. Every accepted token authenticates MCP HTTP, browser WebSocket
connections and the private Vim/broker protocol. Keep these tokens
private.

By default the TCP listener binds IPv4 loopback on the name-derived port.
C<bind> in C<[instance NAME]> or C<--bind> overrides the address and port.

C<--stdio>, or C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_MULTIBROKER_PROTOCOL=stdio>, additionally
serves MCP over stdin/stdout. The TCP listener remains active. In this mode
the process exits after stdio closes and pending responses have drained.

C<--print-url> prints the URL prefix (without a Vim target name) and
exits, without opening the listener. Normal startup does not print
the credential. Use the same C<HOME> and C<XDG_CONFIG_HOME> as the
running broker, for example when checking the URL of a systemd service
account. Changing the global master secret changes derived tokens and
invalidates URLs using those tokens. Explicitly configured tokens are
independent of the master secret.

=head1 PERMISSIONS

MCP tool calls require permission independently of connection authentication.
C<read> covers C<targets>, C<state>, C<read-lines> and C<grep>.
C<write> covers C<apply-diff>, C<substitute>, C<undo>, C<redo> and C<set-cursor>, including
changes to the visible editor position. C<save> authorizes the C<save> tool and is
independent of C<read> and C<write>. C<open> independently authorizes loading
files by pathname. C<exec> authorizes the unrestricted
C<ex> tool. Neither C<read> nor C<write> implies C<exec>, or each other.
Explicit all-action rules also match C<exec>. Dedicated
editing tools leave buffers unsaved; C<save> saves explicitly, and Ex
commands can save files or perform other arbitrary work.

Permission prompts offer the selected editor (all its buffers), or all
editors including future connections. Listing target names has its own
scope. Rules identify editors by their assigned target names, not connection
IDs; a saved rule also applies to a later editor using that name.
Buffer filenames do not restrict these grants.

C<apply-diff> attaches display-only C<View patch> details containing the
editor name, buffer number and supplied diff. YAD offers a separate viewer;
terminal and native MCP prompts show a bounded preview inline. These details
do not narrow a saved editor-wide write grant.

Rules are stored in
C<$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/anyevent-mcp/rules/vim-NAME.ini>, or the corresponding
C<$HOME/.config> path. C<NAME> is the service-instance name. For example,
C<mcp-vim> uses C<rules/vim-default.ini>. Subjects are C<target:TARGET>
for read/write access to an editor and C<targets> for discovery. Ex subjects
are C<ex:JSON_TARGET:JSON_COMMAND>, where both JSON values are strings.
The helper offers exact and editor-wide matchers; there is no need to
write their quoting by hand. An omitted subject still matches all subjects.
An allow rule needs an empty C<sandbox-grants> value:

   [rule]
   decision=allow
   action=read
   subject=target:my-editor
   sandbox-grants=

By default, questions go to the local permission helper. C<--ask tty> and
C<--ask yad> force the built-in terminal or YAD interface without fallback.
Use C<--ask mcp>
to ask through a client with form elicitation, or C<--ask auto> to use
elicitation when advertised and the helper otherwise. A declined elicitation
does not fall back to the helper. The environment variable
C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_ASK> supplies the default when C<--ask> is
omitted. See L<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions> for rule and prompt details.

These checks apply to MCP tools over both HTTP and stdio. They do not change
the authenticated private Vim connection or the browser session protocol.
Reads can still load explicitly addressed buffers and trigger Vim's usual
loading behaviour; these permissions do not sandbox Vim.

=head1 OPTIONS

Common server options: L<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Simple/mcp_getoptions>.
Here C<--bind> and C<--token> also govern private Vim/WebSocket connections;
C<--stdio> and C<--connect> retain that listener. C<--print-url> reports
the credential-bearing URL prefix without Vim target. C<--tool-help> lists
registered tools without starting MCP listener. For example,
C<--auto-allow '*,write'> allows buffer edits, but not C<save>,
C<open> or unrestricted C<exec>.

=over

=item --ask MODE

Permission prompts: C<helper> (default), C<tty>, C<yad>, C<mcp> or
C<auto>. tty/yad force local interface; mcp delegates to client,
auto prefers native elicitation when offered. See L</PERMISSIONS>.

=back

=head1 SYSTEMD

A service can run C</path/to/mcp-vim> directly. Set its C<HOME> and, if
needed, C<XDG_CONFIG_HOME> so it uses the intended global secret.
Run C<mcp-vim --print-url> as the service user to obtain the connection URL;
there is no separate install-time cookie generation step.

=cut

use common::sense;

use Getopt::Long ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Config ();

use MIME::Base64 ();

use Socket ();
use POSIX ();

use JSON::XS ();

use AnyEvent ();
use AnyEvent::Socket ();
use AnyEvent::MCP ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Extension ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::HTTP ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Toolset ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Simple qw(mcp_getoptions mcp_tool_help mcp_log mcp_log_request);

our $PORT;
our $NICK;

my $usage = "Usage: mcp-vim [OPTION]...\n";
my $server_options = mcp_getoptions ({
   usage => $usage,
   path => "/",
   description => "Bridge live Vim buffers to MCP and browser WebSocket clients.\n"
      . "With --stdio, the broker also serves MCP on stdin/stdout; TCP stays active.",
   options => [["--ask MODE", "Ask for permission via helper (default), tty, yad, mcp, or auto"]],
   examples => "  mcp-vim --name editor --bind 127.0.0.1:20792\n"
      . "  mcp-vim --name editor --print-url\n"
      . "  mcp-vim --auto-allow '*,write'",
}, "ask=s" => \my $ask);
@ARGV and die $usage;

my $name = $server_options->{name};
my $bind = $server_options->{bind};
my @tokens = @{$server_options->{tokens} || []};
my $connect = $server_options->{connect};
my $stdio = $server_options->{stdio} || !defined $connect && $ENV{PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_MULTIBROKER_PROTOCOL} eq "stdio";
!($stdio && defined $connect) or die "Cannot combine --stdio and --connect\n";

# private Vim/broker wire protocol (separate from the browser protocol)
my $vim_prot_major = 1;
my $vim_prot_minor = 0;

my $config = AnyEvent::MCP::Config->global;
my %http = $config->http_settings ($name, $bind);
my $host = $http{host};
$PORT = $http{port};

# A URL credential must survive service restarts and --print-url.
my $stored = AnyEvent::MCP::Config->new (file => $config->{file});
$stored->{section}{global}{secret} eq $config->{section}{global}{secret}
   or die "mcp-vim: MCP master secret is not persistently stored in $config->{file}\n";

$http{tokens} = \@tokens if @tokens;
my @accepted_tokens = @{$http{tokens}};
/\A[A-Za-z0-9_.~-]+\z/ or die "Invalid mcp-vim token\n" for @accepted_tokens;
my %accepted_token = map { $_ => 1 } @accepted_tokens;

$NICK //= (POSIX::uname)[1] || die "cannot determine mcp-vim hostname\n";


our $JSON_CODER = JSON::XS->new->utf8;

#############################################################################

my %xpcse;
my %vim;
my %target;

sub targets_changed {
   $_->send_json ([targets => keys %target]) for values %xpcse;
}

#############################################################################

sub base::emit {
   my ($self, $type, @args) = @_;

   eval {
      $type =~ y/-/_/;
      $type = "_on_$type";

      $self->$type (@args);
   };
   if ($@) {
      warn "error executing $type callback: $@";
   }
}

#############################################################################
# vim

@vim::ISA = base::;

sub vim::new {
   my ($class, $hdl, $hello) = @_;

   my ($requested, $instance_id) = @$hello[4, 5];

   (my $old) = grep
      defined $instance_id
      && defined $_->{instance_id}
      && $_->{requested} eq $requested
      && $_->{instance_id} eq $instance_id,
      values %vim;

   my $name = $old ? $old->{target} : $requested;
   $old->destroy if $old;

   unless ($old) {
      my $n = 2;
      $name = "$requested #" . $n++
         while exists $target{$name};
   }

   my $self = bless {
      ah          => $hdl,
      target      => $name,
      requested   => $requested,
      instance_id => $instance_id,
   }, $class;

   $vim{$self+0} = $self;

   AE::log debug => "new vim connection\n";

   # the line-based hello reader leaves subsequent bytes in the handle.
   $hdl->{json} = AnyEvent::MCP::json_class ()->new->utf8->max_depth (512);
   $hdl->rtimeout (0);

   {
      Scalar::Util::weaken (my $self = $self);

      $hdl->on_error (sub {
         $_[0]->destroy;
         $self->destroy;
      });
   }

   mcp_log "vim <$self->{target}> connected";
   $target{$self->{target}} = $self;
   targets_changed;

   $self->read_json;
   $self
}

sub vim::read_json {
   my ($self) = @_;

   Scalar::Util::weaken (my $weak_self = $self);

   $self->{ah}->push_read (json => sub {
      my (undef, $packet) = @_;

      my $self = $weak_self or return;

      $self->emit (@{ $packet->[1] });
      $self->read_json;
   });
}

sub vim::destroy {
   my ($self) = @_;

   delete $vim{$self+0}
      or return;

   $self->{ah}->destroy;

   if (my $save = delete $self->{save}) {
      $save->{xpcse}->cancel_save ($save);
   }

   delete $target{$self->{target}};
   targets_changed;

   $_->(0, "vim disconnected")
      for values %{ delete $self->{rpccb} || {} };

   mcp_log "vim <$self->{target}> disconnected";
}

sub vim::send_msg {
   my ($self, $msg) = @_;

   $self->{ah}->push_write (json => [0, $msg]);

   AE::log trace => "$self->{target} < @$msg";#d#
}

sub vim::rpc_ {
   my $cb = pop;
   my ($self, $cmd, @args) = @_;

   my $id = ++$self->{rpcid};
   $self->{rpccb}{$id} = $cb;

   $self->send_msg ([call => $id, $cmd, @args]);
}

sub vim::_on_reply {
   my ($self, $id, $success, $result) = @_; # also backtrace, but we completelky ignore that

   my $cb = delete $self->{rpccb}{$id}
      or die "reply for id $id has no callback\n";

   $cb->($success, $result);
}

# startup session info
sub vim::_on_session {
   my ($self, $id, $state) = @_;

   $self->{session}{$id} = $state;
}

sub vim::_on_ack {
   my ($self, $bytes) = @_;
}

# end of session list
sub vim::_on_sessions_done {
   my ($self) = @_;

   $self->send_msg ([echo => "connected as '$self->{target}'"]);
}

sub vim::_on_save {
   my ($self, $id, $force) = @_;

   # a save's input belongs to Vim until eof; the browser queue owns the upload.
   if (my $old = delete $self->{save}) {
      $old->{xpcse}->cancel_save ($old);
   }

   my $hdrs = $self->{session}{$id}
      or return $self->send_msg ([echo => "$id: session not found"]);

   my ($xpcse) = grep $_->{origin} eq $hdrs->{origin}, values %xpcse;

   $xpcse
      or return $self->send_msg ([echo => "$id: no origin found, connect a tab for $hdrs->{origin}"]);

   AE::log info => "save $id $hdrs->{origin}";
   $self->send_msg ([echo => "requesting save to $hdrs->{origin}..."]);

   $self->{save} = $xpcse->save ($self, $id, $hdrs, $force);
}

sub vim::_on_data {
   my ($self, $encoded) = @_;

   my $data = MIME::Base64::decode_base64 $encoded;

   if (my $save = $self->{save}) {
      push @{$save->{data}}, $data;
      $save->{xpcse}->send_data;
   } else {
      # discard a rejected save's input without holding up its sender.
      $self->send_msg ([ack => length $data]);
   }
}

sub vim::_on_eof {
   my ($self, $success) = @_;

   my $save = delete $self->{save}
      or return;

   if ($success) {
      $save->{eof} = 1;
      $save->{xpcse}->send_data;
   } else {
      $save->{xpcse}->cancel_save ($save);
   }
}

sub vim::_on_close {
   my ($self, $id) = @_;

   my $hdrs = $self->{session}{$id}
      or return $self->send_msg ([echo => "$id: session not found"]);

   AE::log debug => "vim session close $id";

   unlink $hdrs->{path};
   delete $self->{session}{$id};
}

sub vim::session_open {
   my ($self, $hdrs) = @_;

   my $id = "s" . substr rand, 2;
   $self->{session}{$id} = $hdrs;

   AE::log debug => "vim session open: $id $hdrs->{path}";

   my $filename = $hdrs->{filename};

   $filename =~ s/->/➔/g;
   $filename =~ s/::/∷/g;
   $filename =~ s/\//∕/g;
   $filename =~ s/[\/<>:"\\|\?\*\x00-\x1f]/_/g;

   $self->send_msg ([open => "$id", $filename // "unknown", $hdrs->{extension}, $hdrs]);
}

sub vim::_on_quit {
   my ($self) = @_;

   $self->destroy;
}

#############################################################################

@xpcse::ISA = base::;

sub xpcse::new {
   my ($class, $self) = @_;

   bless $self, $class;

   {
      Scalar::Util::weaken (my $self = $self);

      $self->{conn}->on (finish => sub {
         #AE::log trace => "on finish";#d#
         $self->finish;
      });

      $self->{conn}->on (each_message => sub {
         eval {
            my $opcode = $_[1]->opcode;

            if ($opcode == 1) {
               AE::log trace => "websocket recv: " . $_[1]->body;

               $self->emit (@{ $JSON_CODER->decode ($_[1]->body) });

            } else {
               my $data = $_[1]->body;
               $self->{ondata_cb}($self, $data);
               $self->send_json ([ack => length $data]);
            }
         };
         if ($@) { # AnyEvent::WebScoket simply eats errors and silently disconnects
            warn $@;
            exit 1;
         }
      });

      $self->{pinger} = AE::timer 5, 10 * 60, sub {
         # send regular pong frames, to satisfy nginx and to probe the connection
         $self->{conn}->send (AnyEvent::WebSocket::Message->new (opcode => 10, body => ""));
      };
   }

   $xpcse{$self+0} = $self; # keep alive

   $self->send_json ([nick => $NICK]);

   targets_changed;

   $self
}

sub xpcse::_on_xpcse_client {
   my ($self, $version) = @_;

   if ($version != 1) {
      AE::log warn => "ws xpcse client version mismatch (me 1, them $version)";
      $self->destroy;
      return;
   }
}

sub xpcse::_on_open {
   my ($self, $target, $hdrs) = @_;

   if (my $vim = $target{$target}) {
      $self->{upload_vim} = $vim;

      $vim->session_open ($hdrs);

      $self->{ondata_cb} = sub {
         $vim->send_msg ([data => MIME::Base64::encode_base64 $_[1], ""]) if $vim{$vim+0};
      };
   } else {
      $self->{ondata_cb} = sub { };
   }
}

sub xpcse::_on_eof {
   my ($self, $success) = @_;

   if (my $vim = delete $self->{upload_vim}) {
      $vim->send_msg ([eof => $success*1]) if $vim{$vim+0};
   }

   delete $self->{ondata_cb};
}

sub xpcse::send_json {
   $_[0]{conn}->send (AnyEvent::WebSocket::Message->new (opcode => 1, body => $JSON_CODER->encode ($_[1])));
}

sub xpcse::send_bin {
   $_[0]{conn}->send (AnyEvent::WebSocket::Message->new (opcode => 2, body => $_[1]));
}

sub xpcse::destroy {
   my ($self, $code) = @_;

   delete $xpcse{$self+0}
      or return;

   $code ||= 1002;

   delete $self->{pinger};

   $self->_on_eof (0);
   for my $save (@{delete $self->{saves} || []}) {
      if (my $vim = $save->{vim}) {
         delete $vim->{save}
            if $vim->{save} && $vim->{save} == $save;
         $vim->send_msg ([echo => "$save->{session}: browser disconnected"]) if $vim{$vim+0};
      }
   }

   my $conn = delete $self->{conn};

   $conn->close ($code) if $conn;

   AE::log debug => "websocket connection destroy $code";
}

sub xpcse::finish {
   shift->destroy (@_);
}

sub xpcse::save {
   my ($self, $vim, $session, $hdrs, $force) = @_;

   my $save = {
      xpcse   => $self,
      vim     => $vim,
      session => $session,
      hdrs    => $hdrs,
      force   => $force ? 1 : 0,
      data    => [],
   };

   # the queue and Vim input own the save; neither endpoint is kept alive by it.
   Scalar::Util::weaken ($save->{xpcse});
   Scalar::Util::weaken ($save->{vim});
   push @{$self->{saves}}, $save;
   $self->send_data;
   $save
}

sub xpcse::send_data {
   my ($self) = @_;

   my $save = $self->{saves}[0]
      or return;

   unless (exists $save->{credit}) {
      $save->{credit} = 10e6;
      # Trailing flag is ignored by current browser clients.
      $self->send_json ([upload => $save->{hdrs}, $save->{force}]);
   }

   # a positive credit admits one whole chunk, even if it exceeds the window.
   while (@{$save->{data}} && $save->{credit} > 0) {
      my $data = shift @{$save->{data}};
      $save->{credit} -= length $data;
      $self->send_bin ($data);
   }

   # eof is 1 when Vim has finished, 2 once the browser has been told.
   if ($save->{eof} == 1 && !@{$save->{data}}) {
      $save->{eof} = 2;
      $self->send_json (["upload-done"]);
   }
}

sub xpcse::_on_ack {
   my ($self, $bytes) = @_;

   my $save = $self->{saves}[0]
      or return;

   $bytes =~ /\A[0-9]+\z/ && $bytes <= 10e6 - $save->{credit}
      or return $self->destroy;

   $save->{credit} += $bytes;

   # ack means receipt by the browser, independently of the final save result.
   my $vim = $save->{vim};
   $vim->send_msg ([ack => $bytes]) if $vim && $vim{$vim+0};

   $self->send_data;
}

sub xpcse::_on_upload_done {
   my ($self, $status, $msg, $etag) = @_;

   my $save = $self->{saves}[0]
      or return;

   $save->{eof} == 2
      or return $self->destroy;

   shift @{$self->{saves}};

   my $vim = $save->{vim};
   if ($vim && $vim{$vim+0}) {
      if ($status == 200 && defined $etag && $etag =~ /\A"sha256:[a-f0-9]{64}"\z/) {
         if (my $state = $vim->{session}{$save->{session}}) {
            $state->{etag} = $etag;
            $vim->send_msg (["update-metadata" => $save->{session}, { etag => $etag }]);
         }
      }
      if ($status != 200 || $msg =~ /\n/) {
         $msg =~ s/^upload ok, but.*:\n+//;
         $msg =~ s/\n\n+/\n/g;
         $msg =~ s/\n\z//;
         $vim->send_msg ([error => "error: " . ($status == 200 ? "" : "HTTP $status: ") . $msg]);

         if ($msg =~ / line ([0-9]+), /) {
            $vim->send_msg ([goto => $save->{session}, $1, 1]);
         }

         AE::log warn => "upload error: $status $msg";
      } else {
         $vim->send_msg ([echo => "$status $msg"]);
      }
   }

   # the browser's reply, rather than sending eof, releases the next upload.
   $self->send_data;
}

sub xpcse::cancel_save {
   my ($self, $save) = @_;

   if ($self->{saves}[0] == $save) {
      # there is no upload-abort message; closing prevents a partial POST.
      $self->destroy (1011);
   } else {
      @{$self->{saves}} = grep $_ != $save, @{$self->{saves}};
   }
}

#############################################################################

my $permissions = AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions->new (
   name    => "vim-$name",
   actions => {
      read  => "Read Vim target names, editor state and buffer contents",
      open  => "Load a file into a Vim buffer; autocommands and modelines may run",
      write => "Change Vim buffer contents or the editor position",
      save  => "Save a Vim buffer to its current filename if modified",
      exec  => "Execute arbitrary Vim commands with full host-user privileges",
   },
   (defined $ask ? (ask => $ask) : ()),
   auto_rules => [@{$server_options->{auto_rules} || []},
      $config->auto_rules ($name)],
);

sub vim_permission {
   my ($req, $action, $name, $command, $details, $reason) = @_;

   my $subject = defined $name ? "target:$name" : "targets";
   my $label = defined $name ? "editor '$name' (all buffers)" : "the list of connected editors";
   my @subjects = (
      { label => $label, match => $subject },
      { label => "all Vim editors, including future connections", match => "^" },
   );

   if (defined $command) {
      my $json = JSON::XS->new->allow_nonref;
      my $editor = "ex:" . $json->encode ($name) . ":";
      $subject = $editor . $json->encode ($command);
      @subjects = (
         { label => "this exact command in editor '$name' (all buffers)", match => $subject },
         { label => "any Ex command in editor '$name' (all buffers)", match => "^" . (quotemeta $editor) },
         { label => "any Ex command in any Vim editor, including future connections", match => "^" },
      );
   }

   $req->require_permission ({
      tool           => $req->{tool},
      action         => $action,
      dimensions     => { subject => { label => "Editor / command", value => $subject, choices => \@subjects } },
      sandbox_grants => [],
      reason         => $reason // ("$req->{tool}: " . (defined $name ? "Vim editor '$name'" : "list connected Vim editors")
         . (defined $command ? "\n\n$command" : "")),
      (defined $details ? (details => $details) : ()),
   });
}

my $toolset = AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Toolset->new;
my $json = JSON::XS->new;

sub rpc_reply {
   my ($req, $format) = @_;

   sub {
      my ($success, $result) = @_;

      return if $req->{finished};

      eval {
         $success or die "$result\n";

         $req->reply ($format ? $format->($result) : {
            content => [{
               type => "text",
               text => $json->encode ($result),
            }],
         });
         1
      } or do {
         my $error = $@;

         return if $req->{cancelled};
         die $error if $req->{finished};

         AE::log info => "mcp-tool $req->{tool}: $error";
         $req->error ($error);
      };
   }
}

$toolset->tool (targets =>
   "List connected Vim targets (human-readable editor/session IDs). Use if target unknown.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {},
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      vim_permission $req, read => undef;

      AE::log debug => "vim targets";

      $req->reply ({
         content => [{
            type => "text",
            text => $json->encode ([sort keys %target]),
         }],
      });
   }
);

$toolset->tool (state =>
   "Get live Vim state; optional target selects one editor, omit for all. "
      . "Returns target => state: cwd, current buffer/cursor, buffer metadata. "
      . "Unloaded buffers have null changedtick/line_count; read/search before guarded edits. "
      . "Explicit buffer access loads on demand. "
      . "Call state each turn if current editor/buffer unknown. "
      . "Buffers are live editor state, not disk snapshots.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Optional Vim target id from targets. Omit for all targets.",
         },
      },
      required => [],
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      my @log = length $args->{target} ? $args->{target} : ();

      # keep log values copied: stringifying request scalars can change JSON::XS number/string encoding.
      AE::log debug => join " ", "vim state", @log;

      my @targets = length $args->{target}
         ? $args->{target}
         : keys %target;

      # permission retries must happen before any of the parallel RPCs start.
      vim_permission $req, read => $_ for @targets;
      vim_permission $req, read => undef unless @targets;

      my %state;
      my $pending = @targets;
      my $reply = rpc_reply $req;

      return $reply->(1, \%state) unless $pending;

      for my $name (@targets) {
         my $vim = $target{$name}
            or die "target does not exist: $name\n";

         $vim->rpc_ (state => sub {
            my ($success, $result) = @_;

            return $reply->(0, $result) unless $success;

            $state{$name} = $result;
            $reply->(1, \%state) unless --$pending;
         });
      }
   }
);

$toolset->tool (read_lines =>
   "linenums=true prefixes absolute line number and colon. " .
   "Read live Vim ranges; load explicit buffer on demand. Returns JSON changedtick/range metadata, then two MCP text items per range: [range=start,end], newline-terminated lines.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Vim target id.",
         },
         buffer => {
            type        => "integer",
            description => "Buffer id from state.",
         },
         ranges => {
            type        => "array",
            description => "One or more restricted Vim Ex ranges: %, ., $, positive line numbers, +/- offsets, /regex/, ?regex?, and optional , or ; between two addresses.",
            minItems    => 1,
            items       => { type => "string" },
         },
         linenums => {
            type        => "boolean",
            default     => JSON::XS::false,
            description => "Prefix each line with its absolute line number and a colon.",
         },
      },
      required => ["target", "buffer", "ranges"],
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      vim_permission $req, read => $args->{target};

      my @log = ($args->{target}, $args->{buffer}, @{$args->{ranges}});

      # keep log values copied: stringifying request scalars can change JSON::XS number/string encoding.
      AE::log debug => join " ", "vim read-lines", @log;

      $target{$args->{target}}->rpc_ (
         read_lines => $args->{buffer}, $args->{ranges}, rpc_reply ($req, sub {
            my ($result) = @_;

            my @ranges = @{$result->{ranges}};
            my $metadata = {
               changedtick => $result->{changedtick},
               ranges      => [map {
                  +{
                     range => $_->{range},
                     start => $_->{start},
                     end   => $_->{end},
                  }
               } @ranges],
            };

            +{
               content => [
                  {
                     type => "text",
                     text => $json->encode ($metadata),
                  },
                  map {{
                     type => "text",
                     text => "[range=$_->{start},$_->{end}]",
                  }, {
                     type => "text",
                     text => do {
                        my $line = $_->{start};
                        join "", map { ($args->{linenums} ? $line++ . ":" : "") . "$_\n" } @{$_->{lines}};
                     },
                  }} @ranges,
               ],
            }
         })
      );
   }
);

$toolset->tool (grep =>
   "Search Vim buffers; load explicit IDs on demand; empty buffers = all loaded buffers. "
      . "mode=regex: native Vim regex; mode=word: literal whole word. "
      . "Returns buffer/tick/line/column/text matches, truncated flag.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Vim target id.",
         },
         buffers => {
            type        => "array",
            description => "Buffer IDs; explicit IDs load on demand; [] = all loaded buffers.",
            items       => { type => "integer" },
         },
         pattern => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Native Vim regex (mode=regex) or literal word (mode=word).",
         },
         mode => {
            type        => "string",
            enum        => ["regex", "word"],
            description => "Search syntax.",
         },
         max_results => {
            type        => "integer",
            minimum     => 1,
            description => "Max matches.",
         },
      },
      required => ["target", "buffers", "pattern", "mode", "max_results"],
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      vim_permission $req, read => $args->{target};

      my @buffers = map 0 + $_, @{$args->{buffers}};
      my $log_buffers = @buffers ? (join ",", map 0 + $_, @buffers) : "*";
      my @log = (
         $args->{target},
         $log_buffers,
         $args->{mode},
         (substr $args->{pattern}, 0, 20),
      );

      # keep log values copied: stringifying request scalars can change JSON::XS number/string encoding.
      AE::log debug => join " ", "vim grep", @log;

      $target{$args->{target}}->rpc_ (
         grep => \@buffers, $args->{pattern}, $args->{mode}, $args->{max_results}, rpc_reply ($req)
      );
   }
);

sub normalize_udiff {
   my $diff = shift;
   my @line = split /\n/, $diff, -1;
   my $delta = 0;
   my $hunks = 0;
   my @max_offset;

   pop @line while @line and !length $line[-1];

   for (my $i = 0; $i < @line; ++$i) {
      next unless $line[$i] =~ /^@@ /;

      $line[$i] =~ /^@@ -(\d+)(?:,\d+)?(?: \+\d+(?:,\d+)?)? @@(.*)$/
         or die "normalize_udiff: bad hunk header '$line[$i]'\n";

      my ($old_start, $suffix) = ($1, $2);
      my ($old_count, $new_count) = (0, 0);
      my %old_line;
      my $j = $i + 1;

      # GNU patch mishandles hunks with more prefix than suffix context.
      # Make the first context line an identity change without losing it.
      # see https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/414542/does-patch-work-with-asymmetric-context
      splice @line, $i + 1, 1, "-$1", "+$1"
         if $line[$i + 1] =~ /^ (.*)\z/;

      for (; $j < @line; ++$j) {
         last if $line[$j] =~ /^@@ /;

         my $tag = substr $line[$j], 0, 1;

         if ($tag eq " ") {
            ++$old_count;
            ++$new_count;
            $old_line{substr $line[$j], 1} = 1;
         } elsif ($tag eq "-") {
            ++$old_count;
            $old_line{substr $line[$j], 1} = 1;
         } elsif ($tag eq "+") {
            ++$new_count;
         } elsif ($line[$j] eq "\\ No newline at end of file") {
            # Marker belongs to the preceding body line and counts neither side.
         } else {
            die "normalize_udiff: bad hunk line '$line[$j]'\n";
         }
      }

      $old_count or $new_count
         or die "normalize_udiff: empty hunk at old line $old_start\n";

      $old_count or !$old_start
         or die "normalize_udiff: hunk " . ($hunks + 1)
              . " needs old-side context for insertion after old line $old_start\n";

      my $new_start = $old_start + $delta;
      ++$new_start unless $old_count;
      --$new_start unless $new_count;

      $new_start >= 0
         or die "normalize_udiff: invalid new line $new_start\n";

      $line[$i] = sprintf "@@ -%d,%d +%d,%d @@%s",
         $old_start, $old_count, $new_start, $new_count, $suffix;

      $delta += $new_count - $old_count;
      ++$hunks;
      my $max_offset = keys %old_line;
      --$max_offset
         if $max_offset;
      push @max_offset, $max_offset;
      $i = $j - 1;
   }

   $hunks
      or die "normalize_udiff: no hunks\n";

   ((join "\n", @line, ""), \@max_offset)
}

$toolset->tool (open =>
   "Load pathname into Vim buffer without selecting; reuse existing buffer without rereading. "
      . "Requires separate open permission. Relative paths use Vim's current directory. "
      . "No shell/wildcard/Ex parsing. Missing files create empty named buffers; no save. "
      . "Load autocommands/modelines run: may change editor or fail after side effects. "
      . "Returns buffer, filename, changedtick, line_count, modified.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target => { type => "string", description => "Vim target id." },
         path   => { type => "string", minLength => 1, description => "File pathname, without NUL." },
      },
      required             => ["target", "path"],
      additionalProperties => JSON::XS::false,
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      defined $args->{path} && !ref $args->{path} && length $args->{path} && $args->{path} !~ /\0/
         or die "path must be a nonempty string without NUL\n";
      my $vim = $target{$args->{target}}
         or die "target does not exist: $args->{target}\n";

      vim_permission $req, open => $args->{target}, undef, undef,
         "Open file in Vim editor '$args->{target}'\nPath: " . JSON::XS->new->allow_nonref->encode ($args->{path});

      AE::log debug => "vim open $args->{target} " . $json->encode (substr $args->{path}, 0, 80);

      $vim->rpc_ (open => $args->{path}, rpc_reply ($req));
   }
);

$toolset->tool (apply_diff =>
   "Apply relaxed unified diff to live Vim buffer; reject NUL. In @@ headers only old start (-N) matters: first hunk body line, including context; "
      . "other numbers ignored/recomputed (may be 0); '\@\@ -N \@\@' valid. "
      . "Requires current expected_changedtick and exact context (fuzz=0). Hunk relocation bounded by distinct old-side lines per hunk. Pure insertion only at buffer start; elsewhere include old-side context.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Vim target id.",
         },
         buffer => {
            type        => "integer",
            description => "Buffer id from current Vim state.",
         },
         diff => {
            type        => "string",
            minLength   => 1,
            description => "Unified diff: \@\@ old start (-N) is first hunk body line including context; other counts ignored (may be 0); \@\@ -N \@\@ valid. Pure insertion only at start; elsewhere old-side context required. Hunk relocation bound derived from distinct old-side lines.",
         },
         expected_changedtick => {
            type        => "integer",
            description => "changedtick from latest state/read of this buffer.",
         },
      },
      required => ["target", "buffer", "diff", "expected_changedtick"],
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      defined $args->{diff} && !ref $args->{diff} && $args->{diff} !~ /\0/
         or die "diff must be text without NUL\n";

      my $buffer = $args->{buffer};
      vim_permission $req, write => $args->{target}, undef, {
         "View patch" => "Editor: $args->{target}\nBuffer: $buffer\n\n$args->{diff}",
      };

      my ($hunk, $body);

      for (split /\n/, $args->{diff}) {
         if (/^@@ /) {
            ++$hunk;
         } elsif ($hunk) {
            ++$body;
         }
      }

      my @log = ($args->{target}, $args->{buffer}, $body);

      # keep log values copied: stringifying request scalars can change JSON::XS number/string encoding.
      AE::log debug => join " ", "vim apply-diff", @log;

      $target{$args->{target}}->rpc_ (
         apply_patch =>
            $args->{buffer},
            (normalize_udiff $args->{diff}),
            $args->{expected_changedtick}, rpc_reply ($req)
      );
   }
);

$toolset->tool (substitute =>
   "Guarded Vim :substitute over live buffer range; for mechanical regex replacements. "
      . "Prefer apply-diff for structural/multiline edits. Native Vim regex pattern. "
      . "Native Vim :substitute replacement/flags. "
      . q{To clear the whole buffer, use range %, pattern \%^\_.*\%$, replacement empty, flags empty; Vim leaves one empty line. }
      . "expected_changedtick must match; otherwise stale error.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Vim target id.",
         },
         buffer => {
            type        => "integer",
            description => "Buffer id.",
         },
         range => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Restricted Vim Ex range: %, ., $, positive line numbers, +/- offsets, /regex/, ?regex?, and optional , or ; between two addresses.",
         },
         pattern => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Native Vim regex.",
         },
         replacement => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Native Vim :substitute replacement.",
         },
         flags => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Vim substitute flags; allowed: egiInp#lr.",
            pattern     => "^[egiInp#lr]*\$",
         },
         expected_changedtick => {
            type        => "integer",
            description => "changedtick from latest read/state.",
         },
      },
      required => [
         "target", "buffer", "range", "pattern", "replacement", "flags", "expected_changedtick"
      ],
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      vim_permission $req, write => $args->{target};

      my @log = (
         $args->{target}, $args->{buffer}, $args->{range},
         (substr $args->{pattern}, 0, 20),
         "=>",
         (substr $args->{replacement}, 0, 20),
         $args->{flags},
      );

      # keep log values copied: stringifying request scalars can change JSON::XS number/string encoding.
      AE::log debug => join " ", "vim substitute", @log;

      $target{$args->{target}}->rpc_ (
         substitute => $args->{buffer}, $args->{range}, $args->{pattern},
                       $args->{replacement}, $args->{flags}, $args->{expected_changedtick}, rpc_reply ($req)
      );
   }
);

for my $operation (qw(undo redo update)) {
   $toolset->tool (($operation eq "update" ? "save" : $operation) =>
      $operation eq "update"
         ? "Run Vim :update: save selected buffer to current filename only if modified. "
            . "Requires save permission and current expected_changedtick; no filename/force option. "
            . "Write autocommands run; failure may leave side effects (no rollback). "
            . "Returns buffer, changedtick, modified, filename. "
            . "Preserves caller's current buffer/view when possible."
         : ucfirst ($operation) . " one change in selected Vim buffer via undo tree. "
         . "Requires write permission and latest expected_changedtick. "
         . "Leaves edits unsaved; preserves caller's current buffer when editing another. "
         . "At history boundary: changed=false. "
         . "Returns buffer, changedtick, changed, seq_cur (reached undo sequence), seq_last, modified.",
      {
         type       => "object",
         properties => {
            target => { type => "string", description => "Vim target id." },
            buffer => { type => "integer", minimum => 1, description => "Buffer id from current Vim state." },
            expected_changedtick => {
               type => "integer", minimum => 0,
               description => "changedtick from latest read/state of this buffer.",
            },
         },
         required             => ["target", "buffer", "expected_changedtick"],
         additionalProperties => JSON::XS::false,
      },
      sub {
         my ($req, $args) = @_;

         defined $args->{buffer} && !ref $args->{buffer} && $args->{buffer} =~ /\A[1-9][0-9]*\z/
            or die "buffer must be a positive integer\n";
         defined $args->{expected_changedtick} && !ref $args->{expected_changedtick}
            && $args->{expected_changedtick} =~ /\A[0-9]+\z/
            or die "expected_changedtick must be a nonnegative integer\n";

         my $vim = $target{$args->{target}}
            or die "target does not exist: $args->{target}\n";

         vim_permission $req, ($operation eq "update" ? "save" : "write") => $args->{target};

         AE::log debug => join " ", "vim", ($operation eq "update" ? "save" : $operation),
            $args->{target}, $args->{buffer};
         $vim->rpc_ ($operation => 0 + $args->{buffer}, 0 + $args->{expected_changedtick}, rpc_reply ($req));
      }
   );
}

$toolset->tool (set_cursor =>
   "Show/select buffer and position Vim cursor. Unlike read/edit, intentionally changes user's "
      . "editor position. Optional expected_changedtick rejects stale buffer. "
      . "After edits in user's current buffer, move cursor to changed region so user sees it. "
      . "Avoid cursor movement for exploratory reads/searches unless useful.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Vim target id.",
         },
         buffer => {
            type        => "integer",
            description => "Buffer id.",
         },
         line => {
            type        => "integer",
            minimum     => 1,
            description => "1-based line.",
         },
         column => {
            type        => "integer",
            minimum     => 1,
            description => "1-based byte column as used by Vim col().",
         },
         expected_changedtick => {
            type        => "integer",
            description => "Optional buffer changedtick guard.",
         },
      },
      required => ["target", "buffer", "line", "column"],
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      vim_permission $req, write => $args->{target};

      my @log = ($args->{target}, $args->{buffer}, $args->{line}, $args->{column});

      # keep log values copied: stringifying request scalars can change JSON::XS number/string encoding.
      AE::log debug => join " ", "vim set-cursor", @log;

      $target{$args->{target}}->rpc_ (
         set_cursor => @{$args}{qw(buffer line column)},
            (exists $args->{expected_changedtick} ? $args->{expected_changedtick} : ()), rpc_reply ($req)
      );
   }
);

$toolset->tool (ex =>
   "Execute unrestricted Vim Ex in current editor context; needs separate exec permission. "
      . "Can run shell, read/write files, invoke code, exit Vim with user's privileges. "
      . "Prefer dedicated read/edit/navigation tools. "
      . "command: Ex text, including separators/newlines; keep reviewable. "
      . "Optional buffer becomes current; expected_changedtick requires buffer, checked just before execution. "
      . "Persistent grants may cover exact command, any command in this editor, or all editors. "
      . "Returns {output: Vim messages} or Vim error; no rollback of earlier effects. "
      . "Use noninteractive commands; prompts/long runs block Vim; cancellation cannot undo execution.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      properties => {
         target  => { type => "string", description => "Vim target id." },
         command => { type => "string", minLength => 1, description => "Unrestricted Ex command text, without NUL." },
         buffer  => { type => "integer", minimum => 1, description => "Optional buffer to make current before execution." },
         expected_changedtick => {
            type => "integer", minimum => 0,
            description => "Optional changedtick guard for buffer; requires buffer. A stale tick prevents execution.",
         },
      },
      required => ["target", "command"],
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      my $command = $args->{command};
      defined $command && !ref $command && length $command && $command !~ /\0/
         or die "command must be a nonempty string without NUL\n";

      !exists $args->{expected_changedtick} || exists $args->{buffer}
         or die "expected_changedtick requires buffer\n";

      my $vim = $target{$args->{target}}
         or die "target does not exist: $args->{target}\n";

      vim_permission $req, exec => $args->{target}, $command;

      $vim->rpc_ (ex => $command, @{$args}{qw(buffer expected_changedtick)}, rpc_reply ($req));
   }
);

mcp_tool_help ($server_options->{tool_help}, $toolset);

my $instructions = <<'EOF';
   Live Vim buffers, not disk snapshots. Use targets for editors, state for buffer IDs/changedticks.
   Read via read-lines/grep; explicit access may load unloaded buffers.
   open loads pathname without selecting; requires separate open permission.
   apply-diff/substitute/undo/redo modify live buffers; require current expected_changedtick; leave unsaved.
   save writes current filename only if modified; needs save permission and current expected_changedtick.
   set-cursor moves visible editor cursor; use to show edits or navigate.
   ex runs unrestricted Ex with full Vim privileges; requires separate exec permission. Prefer narrow tools.
EOF

my $custom_instructions = $server_options->{custom_instructions}
   // $config->{section}{"instance $name"}{custom_instructions}
   // $config->{section}{"program " . AnyEvent::MCP::Config::program_name ()}{custom_instructions};
my %guidance = (
   instructions       => $instructions,
   short_instructions => "Live Vim buffers: inspect, edit with changedtick guards (unsaved), navigate. Discover tools.",
   auto_instructions  => 1,
   on_request         => \&mcp_log_request,
   (defined $custom_instructions ? (custom_instructions => $custom_instructions) : ()),
);

my $mcp = AnyEvent::MCP::Server::HTTP->new (
   server_info      => { name => "mcp-vim", version => "0" },
   %guidance,
   toolsets         => [$toolset],
   permissions      => $permissions,
   path             => "/",
   tokens           => \@accepted_tokens,
   max_size         => 100e6,
   origins          => ["*"],
   connection_close => 1,
);

my $done = AE::cv;
my $stdio_mcp;

if (defined $connect) {
   require AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Connect;
   $stdio_mcp = AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Connect->new (
      server_info  => $mcp->{server_info},
      %guidance,
      toolsets     => [$toolset],
      permissions  => $permissions,
      max_size     => 100e6,
      connect      => $connect,
      on_close     => sub { $done->send },
   );
   $stdio_mcp->start;
} elsif ($stdio) {
   require AnyEvent::MCP::Server::JSONL;

   binmode STDIN;
   binmode STDOUT;

   $stdio_mcp = AnyEvent::MCP::Server::JSONL->new (
      server_info         => $mcp->{server_info},
      %guidance,
      toolsets            => [$toolset],
      permissions         => $permissions,
      max_size            => 100e6,
      on_connection_close => sub { $done->send },
   );

   $stdio_mcp->accept (\*STDIN, \*STDOUT);
}

#############################################################################

sub accept_json {
   my ($fh) = @_;

   my $hdl = AnyEvent::Handle->new (
      fh       => $fh,
      on_error => sub { $_[0]->destroy },
      on_eof   => sub { $_[0]->destroy },
   );

   AnyEvent::MCP::Extension::accept ($hdl, \@accepted_tokens, sub {
      my ($hdl, $hello) = @_;

      @$hello == 6 && $hello->[0] eq "mcp-vim"
         && defined $hello->[2] && !ref $hello->[2] && $hello->[2] =~ /\A[0-9]+\z/
         && defined $hello->[3] && !ref $hello->[3] && $hello->[3] =~ /\A[0-9]+\z/
         && $hello->[2] == $vim_prot_major && $hello->[3] <= $vim_prot_minor
         && defined $hello->[4] && !ref $hello->[4]
         && defined $hello->[5] && !ref $hello->[5]
         or die "Invalid private Vim hello\n";

      vim->new ($hdl, $hello);
   });
}

#############################################################################

sub accept_ws {
   my ($fh, $peer_host, $peer_port) = @_;

   eval {
      require AnyEvent::WebSocket::Server;
      require AnyEvent::WebSocket::Message;
      1
   } or return AE::log warn => "WebSocket support unavailable: $@";

   # tcp_server has already formatted the numeric address for us.
   my $peer = AnyEvent::Socket::format_hostport $peer_host, $peer_port;

   my $origin;

   my $ws_server = new AnyEvent::WebSocket::Server
      max_payload_size => 1000e6,
      handshake => sub {
         my ($req, $res) = @_;

         if ($req->{resource_name} !~ m%^/([^/]+)/+(.+)\z%s || !$accepted_token{$1}) {
            AE::log notice => "illegal websocket connect from $peer";
            return;
         }

         $origin = $2;

         AE::log info => "websocket connect for $origin";
         #$res->subprotocol ($prot);

         #$res->{subprotocol}
         #   or return AE::log info => "no matching subprotocol";

         $res
      },
   ;

   $ws_server->establish ($fh)->cb (sub {
      if (my $conn = eval { $_[0]->recv }) {
         xpcse->new ({ conn => $conn, origin => $origin });
      }
   });
}

#############################################################################

AnyEvent::Socket::tcp_server $host, $PORT, sub {
   my ($fh, $peer_host, $peer_port) = @_
      or return;

   my $watcher; $watcher = AE::io $fh, 0, sub {
      undef $watcher;

      defined recv $fh, my $magic, 1, Socket::MSG_PEEK
         or return;

      if ($magic eq "[") { # json
         accept_json $fh;
      } elsif ($magic eq "P" or $magic eq "O") { # HTTP POST/OPTIONS / MCP JSON-RPC 2.0
         $mcp->accept ($fh);
      } elsif ($magic eq "G") {
         accept_ws $fh, $peer_host, $peer_port;
      }
   };
};

$done->recv;

